// Floating macOS-style dock: a second waybar instance (waybar-dock.service),
// launched by the same scripts/launch.sh as the menu bar (see config.jsonc for
// the shared output-selection logic) but with this config/dock.css pair.
//
// "name": "dock" is what makes this instance's layer-shell namespace "dock"
// instead of "waybar" (waybar uses the "name" config key verbatim as the
// namespace, no "waybar-" prefix — confirmed with `niri msg -j layers`, do
// not assume otherwise). Without it, this and the menu bar would share a
// namespace and any layer-rule (e.g. the blur rule in linux/niri/config.kdl)
// aimed at one would hit both.
//
// NO "mode": "dock". That waybar mode forces the layer-shell surface onto
// niri's Bottom z-order layer regardless of the "layer" key below — confirmed
// with `niri msg -j layers` (it reported "dock  Bottom" with mode: "dock"
// set, vs. "dock  Top" without it) — and Bottom sits BELOW normal windows in
// wlr-layer-shell, so any maximized/tall window covered the dock almost
// entirely. Plain "layer": "top" is what actually keeps it above windows.
//
// WHY THE DOCK HAS A FIXED LENGTH (a "margin", not a narrower geometry): waybar
// always paints window#waybar edge-to-edge across its position axis — there is
// no "shrink-to-content" option — so an earlier attempt at painting glass
// directly on window#waybar produced a faint full-length band down the edge
// of the screen instead of a centered pill. The obvious-looking fix, make
// window#waybar transparent and put the glass on an inner box (e.g.
// .modules-center) instead, is a dead end: niri's background-effect has no
// alpha-threshold/ignore-alpha key (`niri validate` rejects both), so blur
// applies to the WHOLE layer-surface rectangle regardless of what's painted
// inside it — that would blur a full-width band AND still show a pill inside
// it, which is worse. So instead the surface itself is shortened with top/bottom
// margins below, sized to fit Launchpad + a handful of running windows on this
// 1080px-tall output. Trade-off accepted: wlr/taskbar's length is LIVE (it grows
// with every open window), so this margin is a static guess, not computed — open
// enough windows at once and the taskbar will clip. If that starts happening
// in practice, shrink the top/bottom margins here; do not revert to the
// transparent full-length + inner-box approach ruled out above.
//
// Honest cost of the static length: this is tuned to HUG the icons rather than
// to leave room for growth — at 220px it fits Launchpad plus about three
// running windows, and beyond that wlr/taskbar starts clipping. That is the
// deliberate choice: an earlier, longer value left a tall half-empty glass
// slab below the icons, which read as a stray background panel rather than a
// dock. If clipping becomes the bigger annoyance, grow it by reducing the
// top/bottom margins here — and expect the empty-desktop look to suffer again.
//
// Contents: Launchpad (grid glyph -> fuzzel), then wlr/taskbar for whatever is
// actually running. No tray here — the menu bar already has one (see
// config.jsonc), and macOS's own split is status icons on the menu bar, apps
// on the dock.
//
// PINNED APPS WERE TRIED AND REMOVED — DO NOT RE-ADD THEM WITHOUT SOLVING THIS
// FIRST: an earlier revision added custom/pin-* glyph buttons (Firefox, kitty,
// Files) alongside wlr/taskbar so the dock would show apps even when they
// weren't running. That broke in a way neither a style review nor a config
// read caught — it only showed up on a real screenshot: waybar has no way to
// associate a pinned launcher button with that same app's live toplevel, so a
// pinned app that was ALSO running rendered TWICE — once as a flat glyph, once
// again as wlr/taskbar's real icon. Firefox pinned + Firefox running showed as
// two separate icons in the dock, not one icon with a running indicator like
// real macOS. Seven slots were really five things. On top of the duplication,
// the pinned glyphs (Pango text) and the taskbar's real app icons (looked up
// from the toplevel's app-id via the desktop-file/icon-theme machinery) never
// looked visually uniform anyway. Re-adding pins needs an actual answer to the
// duplication problem first (e.g. suppressing a pin whenever wlr/taskbar
// already shows that app-id), not just re-adding the custom/ modules.
[
    {
        "name": "dock",
        "output": "__set-by-launch.sh__",
        "layer": "top",
        // Left edge, like macOS with the Dock moved to the left. waybar lays
        // a left/right-positioned bar out VERTICALLY on its own — no
        // "orientation" key needed — so modules-center centers down the
        // column rather than across a row.
        "position": "left",
        // No exclusive zone. A layer-shell surface reserves space by default,
        // and the dock sitting on the left edge was reserving its own width
        // across the WHOLE left side — which inset the menu bar's surface, so
        // waybar's background started 64px in instead of at x=0. Measured, not
        // guessed: sampling the bar's pixels showed it darkening the wallpaper
        // only from x=64 rightwards. With this off the dock overlaps windows
        // instead of displacing them, which is fine because it is on the Top
        // layer and draws above them anyway.
        "exclusive": false,
        // "top right bottom left". Top/bottom are what makes the surface
        // dock-sized instead of full-height on this 1080px-tall output; the
        // left value floats it clear of the screen edge. Tuned by screenshot,
        // not computed. See the length comment above.
        "margin": "430 0 430 8",
        // No "width": let the icons size the bar's thickness, same reasoning
        // as the menu bar's comment on why THAT height is set explicitly —
        // here there is nothing but icon-sized modules.
        "spacing": 14,
        "modules-center": [
            "custom/launchpad",
            "wlr/taskbar"
        ],
        "custom/launchpad": {
            "format": "▦",
            "tooltip-format": "Launchpad",
            "on-click": "fuzzel"
        },
        // Running apps. niri advertises wlr-foreign-toplevel-management, which
        // this module reads over (confirmed: `niri msg -j version` responds,
        // and this module populates live below — no compositor-side gap).
        "wlr/taskbar": {
            "icon-size": 28,
            "spacing": 10,
            "tooltip-format": "{title}",
            "on-click": "activate"
        }
    }
]
